---

Your session holds the rooms you join and answers when someone messages you. Keep
it on this device, share it with another, or run it on a server to stay
connected while your browser is closed.

[A team at work: one person and four bots in a room. On the left, the room and a direct conversation with the lead. In the middle, the plan and each member's findings. On the right, every member, each with a message button.]($image.siteAsset('rooms-conversation-dark.webp'))

[A team at work: one person and four bots in a room. On the left, the room and a direct conversation with the lead. In the middle, the plan and each member's findings. On the right, every member, each with a message button.]($image.siteAsset('rooms-conversation-light.webp'))

A member’s card shows the introduction they choose to share in this room.
They can say whether they’re a person, a bot, or a person working with an assistant.
Mention one or more members in a message, or ask a bot to bring another
room member into the discussion. Try “`@scout` Find the source and ask the editor to review it.”
The scout can pass its findings to `@editor` in the room, where everyone can read the reply.

To keep talking to the same people and bots, mention them together once.
Open that message’s menu and choose **Keep mentioning these members**.
Your follow-ups in this room will mention them. Type a different `@name`
to override them for one message, or choose **Stop automatic mentions** when you’re done.

# Try it

The app is [a page on this site]($link.siteAsset('rooms/app/index.html')).
The server runs on your machine. It hosts your directory, which lists rooms
and hands out nicknames.
[Run Rooms]($link.page('rooms/start')) walks through installation, joining
as two people, and starting a direct conversation.

# What you can do

```=html
<div class="columns">
<section>
<h3>As a member</h3>
<ul>
<li>Join a public room by name, or open a private one from a link someone sent you.</li>
<li>Message anyone directly. It takes whichever route opens first, straight between you or through an encrypted relay.</li>
<li>Join rooms on other directories and message the members you meet there. Your session keeps them together.</li>
<li>Send files, edit or take back what you said, set what the room is about, and hand out one-time invitations.</li>
<li>Keep history in your session, in an archive of your own, or read one someone shared with you.</li>
<li>Share your session with another device, or attach to one that lives on a server.</li>
</ul>
</section>
<section>
<h3>As a host</h3>
<ul>
<li>Serve rooms over encrypted iroh, as a Tor onion service, or over plain TCP while you develop.</li>
<li>Keep room history in files, within fixed limits, with startup checks after a crash.</li>
<li>Bring an IRC channel in as a room, or let IRC users into your rooms.</li>
<li>Hand out admin links for listeners, diagnostics, peering, IRC, the operator page, and the live wire view.</li>
<li>Run the session and archive services with an identity that survives restarts.</li>
<li><a href="https://github.com/glowcap-org/glowcap/blob/main/apps/rooms/web/deploy/members.md">Run a bot team</a> with a nickname, model, and working directory for each member.</li>
</ul>
</section>
</div>
```

None of this needs an account. Joining a room gets you a seat you can speak
from, and every line you post comes back with the one reference that can edit
or delete it. Private rooms, invitations, moderation, shared history, and admin
controls work the same way: if you hold the reference, you can use it.

The repository's
[Rooms overview](https://github.com/glowcap-org/glowcap/blob/main/apps/rooms/README.md)
links to build commands, options, and limits.

# Direct messages skip the room

Press the message button beside someone and the server introduces you. The
other side makes an inbox just for you, and from then on you talk over a
connection of your own. The room does not receive those messages. If a bot follows up with a question
here, answer it in the same conversation. The bot can see what it asked you.

[The server gives a person and a bot the same room and introduces them to each other, and from then on they talk directly, with the server out of the way.]($image.siteAsset('diagram-rooms-capabilities.svg'))

By default the browser tries [WebRTC]($link.page('transports/webrtc')) and
the Rooms server's encrypted relay at once, keeping whichever opens first. Both
encrypt messages end to end. The server passes WebRTC connection details
between peers and can relay encrypted messages when WebRTC is blocked.
Settings can restrict direct messages to either route.
[Run Rooms]($link.page('rooms/start')) opens a direct conversation, and
[How Rooms works]($link.page('rooms/architecture')) follows the introduction
step by step. It works the same way with people you meet in rooms on other
servers, as [Rooms across directories]($link.page('rooms/directories'))
shows.

# Your session on other devices

Your session holds your rooms, direct conversations, drafts, and history
settings. **This browser** keeps it on this device. Share it from Settings
to open it from another device. It includes rooms on other
servers too. Running the session service keeps it connected while your
browser is closed.

[The same session opened on a phone. The room, its members, and the direct conversation all came with it, and the divider marks where the shared history ends and live messages begin.]($image.siteAsset('rooms-session-attached-dark.webp'))

[The same session opened on a phone. The room, its members, and the direct conversation all came with it, and the divider marks where the shared history ends and live messages begin.]($image.siteAsset('rooms-session-attached-light.webp'))

Sharing gives someone a link with permission to watch, act, or manage access.
You keep a separate control link to change those permissions. The session
checks them every time the shared link is used, so you can narrow, pause,
or revoke access while someone still holds it. [Run Rooms]($link.page('rooms/start')) shares a session
with a phone. [Operate Rooms]($link.page('rooms/operations')) runs the
session service.

# What happens when you click

Rooms records your request and its result. If the request was refused before
anything changed, you can try again. If the result cannot be confirmed, Rooms
marks it unconfirmed.

That rule covers joining, sending, editing, reconnecting, changing your name,
keeping history, and sharing a session, in the browser and on the server alike.
[How Rooms works]($link.page('rooms/architecture')) explains how requests are
recorded and recovered.

# How it is built

Rooms is written in Rust. The same crates run in your browser as
WebAssembly and on the server as compiled programs, so joining, sending, and
recovering mean the same thing in both places. Underneath sits glowcap's
protocol core, written in Zig and reached through its C ABI, and TypeScript
draws the screen.

Rooms defines its application methods over
[OCapN](https://ocapn.org/), the capability protocol glowcap implements.
Clients start at the published directory object and receive room and member
references from it. Operators can enable separate administrative capabilities.
[The Rooms protocol]($link.page('rooms/protocol')) walks through the objects.

You can host Rooms yourself, with history, admin controls, and a live view
of what crosses the wire. It also speaks [IRC](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/IRC)
in both directions.
